  "summary": "A style based on the architecture of Spanish missions in California and the Southwest United States.",
  "description": "It features smooth stucco walls, red tile roofs, rounded arches, and bell towers, often with minimal ornamentation. This style emerged as part of a broader interest in Spanish colonial heritage and was popularized by the Panama-Pacific International Exposition in San Francisco in 1915.",
  "culturalContext": "Mission Revival (1890s-1910s) romanticized California's Spanish mission churches into a railway-and-suburb style: curvilinear mission-shaped gables, wide verandas, clay tile roofs, arched corbel tables and stucco. The Santa Fe and Southern Pacific railroads built depots and hotels (Fresno's depot, Mission Inn's core) in the style to brand the Southwest; it spread through schools, depots and bungalows across the American West before Churrigueresque flash took over.",
